      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the problem of fully simplifying \\(\\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3}\\) and express it in the form \\(a\\sqrt[4]{b}\\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Express the radicand in terms of factors of 2 and 3.**\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Separate the factors into groups that are easier to handle under the fourth root.**\n   We know that:\n   \\[\n   2^7 = 2^4 \\cdot 2^3 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 3^3 = 3^3\n   \\]\n   Therefore, we can rewrite the expression as:\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3} = \\sqrt[4]{(2^4 \\cdot 2^3) \\cdot 3^3}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Simplify the expression inside the fourth root.**\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt[4]{(2^4 \\cdot 2^3) \\cdot 3^3} = \\sqrt[4]{2^4 \\cdot 2^3 \\cdot 3^3}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Recognize that \\(2^4\\) can be simplified from under the fourth root.**\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt[4]{2^4} = 2^{4/4} = 2^1 = 2\n   \\]\n   So, the expression simplifies to:\n   \\[\n   2 \\cdot \\sqrt[4]{2^3 \\cdot 3^3}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Express the remaining term under the fourth root as it is.**\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt[4]{2^3 \\cdot 3^3} \\quad \\text{cannot be simplified further using a simpler radical form.}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Combine the results.**\n   The simplified form of \\(\\sqrt[4]{2^7 \\cdot 3^3}\\) is:\n   \\[\n   2 \\cdot \\sqrt[4]{2^3 \\cdot 3^3}\n   \\]\n   Here, \\(a = 2\\) and \\(b = 2^3 \\cdot 3^3\\).\n\n7. **Calculate \\(a\\) and \\(b\\).**\n   \\[\n   b = 2^3 \\cdot 3^3 = 8 \\cdot 27\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   a = 2\n   \\]\n\n8. **Sum \\(a\\) and \\(b\\).**\n   \\[\n   a + b = 2 + 8 \\cdot 27\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   8 \\cdot 27 = 216\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   a + b = 2 + 216 = 218\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{218}\n\\]",
